is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: DNS service (BIND) policy on dns_server (10.200.0.2). The routing/switching fabric is healthy; host_1_1_1_1 can reach the servers by IP.

Mechanism: Applications on host_1_1_1_1 stall because DNS lookups are refused, causing retries/timeouts. Direct IP access is fast: curl from host_1_1_1_1 to http://10.200.0.3/–.6 completes in ~2–3 ms, and ICMP to 10.200.0.x is lossless. However, DNS queries from host_1_1_1_1 to 10.200.0.2 return “status: REFUSED” with “recursion requested but not available,” confirming the resolver is disallowing recursive service to that client.

Localization: dns_server, BIND configuration. On host_1_1_1_1, /etc/resolv.conf points to 10.200.0.2. Dig @10.200.0.2 web_server_0 and web_server_1 both return REFUSED. On dns_server, BIND is listening on 10.200.0.2:53 (netstat shows listeners), and /etc/bind/named.conf.options contains only defaults and no allow-recursion/allow-query for remote subnets, so only localhost/localnets are permitted. The host’s subnet (10.1.1.0/24) is not “localnets” of dns_server (which is 10.200.0.0/24).

Root cause: BIND on dns_server is configured to deny recursion to non-local subnets, so clients on 10.1.1.0/24 cannot resolve internal server names.

Corrective fix: On dns_server, update /etc/bind/named.conf.options to enable recursion for all internal networks, e.g.:
- recursion yes;
- allow-recursion { 10.1.1.0/24; 10.2.1.0/24; 10.200.0.0/24; };
- optionally allow-query { any; };
Then reload/restart BIND (rndc reload or service restart). Validate with dig from host_1_1_1_1; name-based access should work immediately. 
=== END ===